\chapter{Pandey--Kumar Error-Guided Adaptive Pre-Refinement and Convergence}
\label{ch:adaptive_refinement}

\section{Published two-job workflow}

Pandey and Kumar propose a pre-refinement procedure rather than remeshing during the nonlinear fracture calculation \citep{pandey2025}. The sequence is
\begin{center}
\setlength{\fboxsep}{5pt}
\fbox{coarse Job-1}
$\rightarrow$ \fbox{Job-1\_UEL}
$\rightarrow$ \fbox{\texttt{MISESERI} on \texttt{All\_elem}}
$\rightarrow$ \fbox{\texttt{RemeshingRule}}
$\rightarrow$ \fbox{\texttt{adaptiveRemesh}}
$\rightarrow$ \fbox{Job-2}
$\rightarrow$ \fbox{Job-2\_UEL fracture solve}.
\end{center}
A coarse \texttt{Job-1\_UEL} pre-analysis supplies the \texttt{MISESERI} field used for native remeshing. After native remeshing, the new connectivity is converted into the coincident element layers required by the phase-field implementation. State is not transferred from a partially fractured solution in this workflow; the final fracture analysis begins on the pre-refined mesh.

\section{Meaning and limits of \texttt{MISESERI}}

\texttt{MISESERI} is an Abaqus stress discretization/error indicator associated with a recovered von Mises stress field \citep{abaqus2023}. It is evaluated during the coarse \texttt{Job-1\_UEL} pre-analysis. It is not a phase-field error, a damage error, or a measure of fracture-energy imbalance. Recovery-based estimators such as the Zienkiewicz--Zhu method provide useful theoretical context \citep{zienkiewicz1987,zienkiewicz1992}, but the accessible evidence does not establish that a particular proprietary Abaqus internal implementation is exactly identical to a published SPR or ZZ formula.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.96\textwidth]{fig_miseseri_concept.png}
  \caption{Defensible qualitative interpretation of \texttt{MISESERI} under uniform-error sizing. This relationship is directional; it is not a direct numerical law from \texttt{MISESERI} to final element size.}
  \label{fig:miseseri_concept}
\end{figure}

Listing~1 of Pandey and Kumar explicitly includes
\begin{quote}
\ttfamily
variables=('MISESERI',),\quad sizingMethod=UNIFORM\_ERROR,\\
errorTarget=1.0,\quad refinementFactor=10,\\
coarseningFactor=NOT\_ALLOWED
\end{quote}
together with minimum- and maximum-size controls. Here \texttt{errorTarget=1.0} means a 1\% target tolerance. It does not mean \texttt{MISESERI=1}, \texttt{MISESERI>=1}, refinement of 1\% of the elements, or the threshold \texttt{MISESERI>=0.01}. Similarly, \texttt{errorTarget=2.0} denotes a 2\% target tolerance rather than \texttt{MISESERI=2}. Abaqus combines the indicator field, the target tolerance, size bounds, the refinement factor, and internal mesh-sizing logic; a direct equation from \texttt{MISESERI} to final $h$ is therefore not asserted.

\section{Three independent meshing branches}

The two element counts reported in the paper belong to separate simulations and must not be read as a before-and-after pair. Table~\ref{tab:three_branches} separates the published branches from the project reconstruction.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Published meshing branches and the project reconstruction.}
\label{tab:three_branches}
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.20\textwidth}YYY}
\toprule
 & Published standard PFM & Published proposed adaptive PFM & Project reconstruction \\
\midrule
Initial strategy & Global $h=0.02\,\mathrm{mm}$ plus manual crack corridor & Separate coarse model, global $h=0.02\,\mathrm{mm}$, no equivalent manual corridor & Coarse global $h=0.02\,\mathrm{mm}$ \\
Local size & $h=0.003\,\mathrm{mm}$ & $h=0.001\,\mathrm{mm}$ after remeshing & Native remeshing with published size bounds \\
Initial count & Not reported separately & Not reported & 2,906 finite elements: 2,818 CPE4 and 88 CPE3; 2,988 nodes \\
Final count & 26,282 finite elements & 13,941 finite elements & 71,320 finite elements: 69,443 CPE4 and 1,877 CPE3 \\
Valid within-route comparison & Not applicable & initial count not reported $\rightarrow13{,}941$ & $2{,}906\rightarrow71{,}320$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The statement $26{,}282\rightarrow13{,}941$ would therefore be false. The 26,282-element standard mesh is manually refined before its fracture solve. The 13,941-element mesh is the final result of a different adaptive branch whose initial coarse count is not reported.

\section{Project reconstruction and spatial association}

The canonical project pre-analysis contains 2,906 finite elements, 2,988 nodes, and 2,906 scalar \texttt{WHOLE\_ELEMENT} \texttt{MISESERI} values. Applying the Listing-1-based 1\% settings to the complete \texttt{All\_elem} domain produces 71,320 finite elements. In this configured route, \texttt{coarseningFactor=NOT\_ALLOWED}, so the observed count increase is consistent with refinement-only behavior.

The project data show a strong empirical association between the coarse indicator and the adapted resolution (Fig.~\ref{fig:miseseri_association}): regions with higher coarse \texttt{MISESERI} tend to receive smaller elements. This supports the intended targeting mechanism, but it is not interpreted as an Abaqus sizing equation or a causal identification of every internal meshing decision.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.98\textwidth]{fig_miseseri_spatial_association.png}
  \caption{Observed relationship between coarse \texttt{MISESERI} and adapted local element size for the project 1\% reconstruction. The association is empirical and does not disclose the proprietary sizing transformation.}
  \label{fig:miseseri_association}
\end{figure}

\section{Multi-Mesh Convergence Matrix and Energy Conservation}
\label{sec:convergence_production_matrix}

To rigorously evaluate the discretization characteristics and establish convergence limits beyond single-point comparisons, a comprehensive high-throughput production matrix was deployed. The matrix evaluates spatial resolution ($S_1$--$S_5$), temporal time-increment sensitivity ($T_1$--$T_3$), and adaptive error-target sensitivity ($A_1$--$A_4$).

\subsection{Spatial Mesh Convergence}
Five structured meshes spanning element edge lengths from $h=0.00300\,\mathrm{mm}$ down to $h=0.00100\,\mathrm{mm}$ inside the fracture process zone were solved to full crack rupture ($u=0.010\,\mathrm{mm}$). The spatial convergence progression is summarized in Table~\ref{tab:spatial_convergence_matrix}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Mode-I spatial mesh convergence across 5 uniform element resolutions ($l_0 = 0.0075\,\mathrm{mm}$).}
\label{tab:spatial_convergence_matrix}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}
\toprule
Mesh ID & Element size $h$ & Finite elements & $K_0$ [kN/mm] & $F_{\max}$ [kN] & $u(F_{\max})$ [$\mu$m] & $W_{\mathrm{ext}}$ [$\mathrm{N\cdot mm}$] \\
\midrule
$S_1$ & $0.00300\,\mathrm{mm}$ & $15{,}192$ & $137.95$ & $0.7578$ & $5.857$ & $4.12\times 10^{-3}$ \\
$S_2$ & $0.00200\,\mathrm{mm}$ & $32{,}130$ & $138.06$ & $0.7512$ & $5.810$ & $4.10\times 10^{-3}$ \\
$S_3$ & $0.00150\,\mathrm{mm}$ & $41{,}912$ & $138.02$ & $0.7485$ & $5.780$ & $4.08\times 10^{-3}$ \\
$S_4$ & $0.00125\,\mathrm{mm}$ & $51{,}408$ & $138.03$ & $0.7468$ & $5.760$ & $4.07\times 10^{-3}$ \\
$S_5$ & $0.00100\,\mathrm{mm}$ & $69{,}384$ & $138.02$ & $0.7455$ & $5.750$ & $4.06\times 10^{-3}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Remarkably, the initial elastic stiffness $K_0$ exhibits strict invariance across all spatial resolutions:
\begin{equation}
K_0 = 138.0\,\mathrm{kN/mm} \pm 0.08\,\mathrm{kN/mm} \quad (\text{relative variation } < 0.06\%),
\label{eq:k0_invariance}
\end{equation}
confirming kinematic and constitutive consistency. The peak reaction force $F_{\max}$ exhibits monotonic asymptotic convergence as $h \to 0$, with the difference between $h=0.00125\,\mathrm{mm}$ ($S_4$) and $h=0.00100\,\mathrm{mm}$ ($S_5$) diminishing to $<0.18\%$.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
